inline.s:
Ten thousand bamboos ring my old retreat;
the books I read through hang upon the wall.
In dreams I see four eaves, their green complete;
only the moon is left to light the hall.

Seven rolls of the Lotus, a stick of incense;
the long day through I sit cross-legged, at rest.
Beyond the gate, the world’s dust, fathomless hence;
let wheels and hooves run busy — that is best.

The ancestral meaning shows on each grass blade;
spring woods in bloom, bird-song in quiet shade.
At dawn the rain has passed, the hills are rinsed;
on red and white boughs hangs the dew unstained.

Heaven makes iron armor my worn fur cloak;
not for a marquis’s title did I take the field.
Through blazing suns like furnace fires I woke;
my proud bones now are forged and made to yield.

Dew falls upon the orchids, fragrance in the air;
the wind blows over flesh and bone, night turning chill.
Sitting, I feel my mind already white as snow;
the moonlight still I take for frost upon the sill.

We met on Yu Ridge when the day was late and slow;
I thought to break a plum spray — which, I could not say.
Then all at once the autumn wind bore me across the burning sea;
and parting is not as it was on that first day.
